On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:17:12 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> lab-process-mail works on a single thread at a time. I don't know if
> that's necessary or useful.

It is one message at a time, in fact, which is a step narrower than a
thread. A thread with three unanswered messages costs three passes,
even though the first pass already read the other two through its
thread reader.

It is not necessary. It came from your spec in "deterministic just
process-email task for claude" [1], and the reasons recorded in "[PATCH
v3 2/6] just: Add just process-email" [2] and "[PATCH 1/6]
src/lab-process-mail: Answer the inbox from outside the tree" [3] were
these three:

1. The model never decides when to stop.
2. Each pass starts with a fresh context, so one context does not grow
   with every thread.
3. The script can check that exactly the message it named left the
   inbox.

Only the third needs one message per pass, and only in its current
form. The check works just as well over a list of ids.

Per-message passes also cut against CLAUDE.md's "Read the whole batch
before working". The thread reader catches corrections later in the
same thread. It misses a message in another thread that supersedes
this one, such as a new RFC that redefines an older ask. A pass that
sees the whole inbox would catch that.

> Would it be better for it to load all of the inbox threads at once, and
> kick off subagents to parallelize them? It wouldn't necessarily
> parallelize every one - but let the primary agent figure that out.

Yes, I think so, as long as the script still owns the loop. This is
what I would build:

- The script brings everything up to date as it does now, then takes
  the oldest N inbox messages, where N is ${LAB_PROCESS_MAIL_BATCH} and
  defaults to 10. It names every id in the prompt.
- The agent reads every message first and works out which ones
  constrain the others. It then fans out read-only subagents, one per
  thread, for research and drafting, and decides which threads can
  share one. It applies the writes in order from the parent, meaning
  the commits in work.jj, the mail commits, and the push. There is one
  work workspace and one mail workspace, and parallel writes to them
  corrupt state.
- Afterwards the script checks each id against tag:inbox, exactly as
  it checks the single id today, and sends one notification per
  message, answered or stuck. If any id is still in the inbox it exits
  1 and lists them. Control still rests only on the notmuch index,
  never on what the model printed.
- The loop runs until the inbox is empty, so mail that arrives during
  a batch goes into the next one. lab-process-mail-loop does not
  change, because it only looks at the exit status.

What it costs:

- Context. The parent holds a summary of every thread in the batch,
  and the cap on N is what keeps that bounded. A batch of ten short
  results should fit comfortably.
- Blast radius. One crash or quota cutoff leaves the whole batch
  unanswered, where today it costs one message. Because the check is
  per id, the partial progress is still visible and exact.
- A reply can no longer be pinned to one fresh pass. If something goes
  wrong in a batch, it is harder to say which thread's work caused it.

What it gains:

- The whole batch is read before any work starts, as described above.
- There is one fetch and rebase per batch instead of one per message.
- Research runs in parallel, so wall-clock time drops. As I said in
  "Re: [RFC] push vs pull for agents" [4], your review is the real
  bottleneck, so this is mostly about coherence and latency, not
  throughput.

A smaller step is to pass every inbox message in the oldest message's
thread to one pass. That fixes the repeated passes per thread but
not the cross-thread case. I would go straight to the batch.

If you agree, the patch would change lab-process-mail, its tests, and
prompt.md (steps 2 and 6 through 8 become per-message within one
pass). I left it unwritten because it replaces the contract the
current prompt is built on, and I would rather you pick the shape
first. Let me know whether you want N capped as above, or the whole
inbox each time.

